"Instella: Fully Open Language Models with Stellar Performance (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.10628v1)\nciteturn2453view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2443view1\",\"lineno\":120}); Total lines: 416\nL111: ### 3.3 Pre-training\nL112: \nL113: Figure 2: Instella-3B model training pipeline.\nL114: We pre-train the model using two stages with a sequence length of 4,096 tokens and a global batch size of 1,024. The Instella 3B pretraining pipeline is shown in Fig. cite76†2 .\nL115: In the first pre-training stage, we train the model from scratch on 4.07 trillion tokens sourced from OLMoE-mix-0924 (cite71†Muennighoff et al., 2025 ), which is a diverse mix of two high-quality datasets DCLM-baseline (cite77†Li et al., 2024 ) and Dolma 1.7 (cite78†Soldaini et al., 2024 ) covering domains like coding, academics, mathematics, and general world knowledge from web crawl. This extensive first stage pre-training established a foundational understanding of general language in our Instella model.\nL116: We use the cosine decay learning rate schedule with a maximum learning rate of $4\\times 10^{-4}$ and set the global batch size to 1024.\nL117: For our final pre-trained checkpoint, Instella-3B, we conduct a second stage pre-training on top of the first-stage Instella-3B-Stage1 model to further enhance its capabilities on MMLU (cite55†Hendrycks et al., 2021b ), BBH (cite54†Suzgun et al., 2023 ), and GSM8K (cite53†Cobbe et al., 2021b ). The model is trained three times with different random seeds, and the resulting weights are ensembled to obtain the final checkpoint.\nL118: Specifically, the second-stage training uses 58 billion tokens sourced from diverse and high-quality datasets, including Dolmino-Mix-1124 (cite51†OLMo et al., 2024 ), SmolLM-Corpus (python-edu) (cite79†Ben Allal et al., 2024 ), Deepmind Mathematics (cite80†Saxton et al., 2019 ), and conversational datasets such as Tülu-3-SFT-Mixture (cite81†Lambert et al., 2024 ), OpenHermes-2.5 (cite82†Teknium, 2023 ), WebInstructSub (cite83†Yue et al., 2024 ), Code-Feedback (cite84†Zheng et al., 2024 ), and Ultrachat 200k (cite85†Ding et al., 2023 ).\nL119: We use the linear decay learning rate schedule with a maximum learning rate of $4\\times 10^{-5}$ and set the global batch size to 1024.\nL120: In addition to the publicly available datasets, 28.5 million tokens in the second-stage pre-training data mixture are derived from our in-house synthetic dataset focused on mathematical problems.\nL121: This dataset is generated using the training set of GSM8k dataset, where we first use Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct (cite40†Yang et al., 2024 ) to 1) abstract numerical values as function parameters and generate a python program to solve the math question, 2) identify and replace numerical values in the existing question with alternative values that are still answerable with the same python program solution as the original question.\nL122: Next, by assigning different new values to these python parameters and using the abstract solution program to compute the corresponding answers, we expand our synthetic dataset with new and reliable question-answer pairs (cite86†Yu et al., 2024 ).\nL123: ### 3.4 Post-training\nL124: We first perform supervised finetuning (SFT) to enable the pre-trained model to follow instructions and respond effectively to user queries. We train for three epochs on 2.3 millions of high-quality instruction–response pairs, resulting in Instella-3B-SFT. During this phase, we utilize datasets spanning a broad spectrum of tasks and domains to ensure that the model generalizes across diverse instruction types.\nL125: The mixture is selectively sourced from SmolTalk (cite52†Allal et al., 2025 ), OpenMathInstruct-2 (cite87†Toshniwal et al., 2024 ), Tülu-3 Instruction Following (cite81†Lambert et al., 2024 ), MMLU auxiliary train set (cite55†Hendrycks et al., 2021b ), and o1-journey (cite88†Qin et al., 2024 ). We use the linear decay learning rate schedule with a maximum learning rate of $1\\times 10^{-5}$ and set the global batch size to 128.\nL126: In the final training stage, we align Instella-3B-SFT with human preferences to ensure its outputs are helpful, accurate, and safe. Building on Instella-3B-SFT, Instella-3B-Instruct is trained with direct preference optimization (DPO) (cite42†Rafailov et al., 2023 ) on 0.76 billion tokens from the OLMo 2 1124 7B Preference Mix (cite51†OLMo et al., 2024 ).\nL127: This alignment step tailors the model’s responses to better reflect human values and expectations, thereby improving the quality and reliability of its outputs. We use the linear decay learning rate schedule with a maximum learning rate of $5\\times 10^{-7}$ and set the global batch size to 128.\nL128: ## 4 Instella-Long\nL129: \nL130: In this section, we introduce the long-context model of Instella, namely, Instella-3B-Long-Instruct, supporting 128K context length. To extend the context length, we continually train the model from Instella-3B-Instruct through: 1. continued pre-training, 2. supervised finetuning (SFT), and 3. direct preference optimization (DPO), as shown in Fig. cite89†3 . We detail the training method and data in the following subsections.\nL131: \nL132: Figure 3: Instella-Long model training pipeline.\nL133: ### 4.1 Continued Pre-training\nL134: The long context training is initialized from the short-context checkpoint, Instella-3B-Instruct, which has a context length of 4K. We conduct a two-stage continued pre-training to gradually increase the context length. Stage 1: We extend the context length from 4K to 64K and train the model using 20B tokens. The batch size is 4M tokens and the training steps are 5,000. We follow the RoPE scaling law (cite59†Gradient Team, 2024 ) to increase the base frequency of RoPE from 10,000 to 514,640.\nL135: We also experiment with alternative RoPE scaling methods (cite60†emozilla, 2023 ; cite62†Gao et al., 2024 ) and observe only minor differences in performance. Stage 2: As indicated by (cite62†Gao et al., 2024 ), it is beneficial to train the model with the data whose context length is longer than the target context length. In this stage, we train the model on 20B tokens with a maximum context length of 256K - twice our target context length of 128K.\nL136: Following the RoPE scaling law, we further increase the RoPE base frequency to 3,691,950. The batch size is 8M tokens and the training steps are 2,500. For both stages, we use the linear decay learning rate schedule and the maximum learning rate is $2\\times 10^{-5}$.\nL137: Table 2: Long-context continued pre-training data by source and portion. Each stage consists of 20 billion tokens in total.\nL138: Training Stage  | 64K Long Data  | 256K Long Data  | Short Data\nL139: Stage 1  | Code repos (30%) Books (30%) Textbooks (3%)  | –  | FineWeb-Edu (10%) FineWeb (10%) Wikipedia (5%) OpenWebMath (5%) StackExchange (4%) ArXiv (3%)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInstella: Fully Open Language Models with Stellar Performance (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.10628v1)\nciteturn2453view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2443view1\",\"lineno\":174}); Total lines: 416\nL161: Document Masking and Data Batching. We apply document masking during the continued pre-training and SFT, as each input sequence may contain multiple documents. Document masking is achieved through variable-length FlashAttention (cite93†Dao, 2023 ), which computes attention within each individual document rather than across the entire sequence. This design can also improve training throughput when combined with sorted data batching.\nL162: Following Prolong (cite62†Gao et al., 2024 ), we sort microbatches at each training step by the sum of document lengths in the sequence. With gradient accumulation, later microbatches benefit from faster processing when they consist of shorter documents.\nL163: ## 5 Instella-Math\nL164: \nL165: Figure 4: Instella-Math model training pipeline.\nL166: \nL167: In this section, we introduce Instella-Math, a reasoning-centric language model trained with long chain-of-thought reinforcement learning. To enhance the model’s mathematical and logical reasoning capabilities, we continually train Instella-3B-Instruct through two stages of supervised finetuning and three stages of reinforcement learning, as shown in Figure cite94†4 . We detail the training procedure and datasets below.\nL168: ### 5.1 Supervised Finetuning\nL169: \nL170: As a cold start, we perform a two-stage supervised finetuning process to enhance the reasoning capabilities of Instella-3B-Instruct:\nL171: Stage 1: Instruction Tuning with OpenMathInstruct-2 for Mathematical Coverage. In the first SFT stage, we begin with instruction tuning, following instructions or prompts properly, especially in a question-answer or problem-solution format.\nL172: Using the OpenMathInstruct-2 dataset (cite87†Toshniwal et al., 2024 ), which consists of 14 million problem-solution pairs generated from the GSM8K (cite53†Cobbe et al., 2021b ) and MATH (cite64†Hendrycks et al., 2021d ) training sets, the model is trained to solve mathematical questions covering a diverse range of topics from arithmetic and algebra to probability and calculus.\nL173: Stage 2: Deep Reasoning with Long-Context Training on AM-DeepSeek-R1-Distilled. In the second SFT stage, we further improve the model’s reasoning capability by training on AM-DeepSeek-R1-Distilled-1.4M (cite95†Zhao et al., 2025 ), which is a large-scale general reasoning dataset containing high-quality and challenging problems.\nL174: In this stage, we increase the context length of the model from 4K to 32K to allow the model to learn from the long chain-of-thought responses distilled from large reasoning models such as DeepSeek-R1 (cite66†DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025 ).\nL175: ### 5.2 Reinforcement Learning\nL176: \nL177: Following supervised finetuning, we apply three stages of reinforcement learning using the group relative policy optimization (GRPO) algorithm (cite44†Shao et al., 2024 ) to further strengthen the model’s mathematical reasoning abilities. Training is orchestrated with verl (cite96†Sheng et al., 2024 ) and vLLM (cite97†Kwon et al., 2023 ) for efficient rollout collection, reward scoring, and policy updates.\nL178: Stage 1: GRPO on Big-Math-RL-Verified (8 Rollouts @ 8K Tokens). In the first stage of reinforcement learning, we apply the GRPO algorithm to train the model on Big-Math-RL-Verified (cite98†Albalak et al., 2025 ), a collection of curated, complex, multi-step math problems. We generate 8 rollouts per prompt, each with up to 8K output tokens, to explore diverse reasoning trajectories.\nL179: The model is trained for 1,200 GRPO steps using rule-based reward signals provided by Prime-RL (cite99†Cui et al., 2025 ), which incentivize correctness and well-structured outputs.\nL180: Stage 2: GRPO on DeepMath (16 Rollouts @ 16K Tokens). To push the limits of long-form reasoning, we conduct a second GRPO stage on DeepMath (cite100†He et al., 2025 ) using 16 rollouts per prompt with up to 16K output tokens. This stage is designed to maximize the model’s capacity for deep mathematical reasoning, enabling it to solve problems that require extended derivations, multiple nested logical steps, or structured proof-like outputs. In this stage, the model is trained for 600 GRPO steps.\nL181: Stage 3: GRPO on DeepScaleR (16 Rollouts @ 16K Tokens). In the final GRPO stage, we finetune the model on DeepScaleR (cite45†Luo et al., 2025 ), which includes original Olympiad math problems (e.g., AIME and AMC). Similar to Stage 2, this training uses 16 rollouts and a 16K token limit. We run 740 GRPO steps in this phase to improve performance on competition-style reasoning tasks.\nL182: ## 6 Evaluation\nL183: ### 6.1 Base Model\nL184: Table 4: Base model performance.\nL185: Models  | ARC-C  | ARC-E  | BoolQ  | HS.  | PiQA  | SciQ  | WG.  | OBQA  | MMLU  | BBH  | GSM8K  | Avg.\nL186: Open Weight Models\nL187: Gemma2-2B  | 39.5  | 59.3  | 74.5  | 70.5  | 76.4  | 96.6  | 69.8  | 44.8  | 53.3  | 40.8  | 27.4  | 59.3\nL188: Llama-3.2-3B  | 47.2  | 64.9  | 74.8  | 73.1  | 75.9  | 95.3  | 70.3  | 51.2  | 57.8  | 47.0  | 30.1  | 62.5\nL189: Qwen2.5-3B  | 51.5  | 67.2  | 79.1  | 72.1  | 77.4  | 95.5  | 69.3  | 51.4  | 67.2  | 56.7  | 63.8  | 68.3\nL190: Fully Open Models\nL191: Pythia-2.8B  | 40.5  | 60.7  | 64.8  | 60.1  | 72.5  | 89.7  | 60.8  | 42.6  | 26.1  | 27.7  | 2.7  | 49.8\nL192: GPTNeo-2.7B  | 38.5  | 54.6  | 62.7  | 55.2  | 70.8  | 88.0  | 58.3  | 40.8  | 27.8  | 27.3  | 3.7  | 48.0\nL193: OpenELM-3B  | 37.5  | 58.4  | 68.6  | 71.7  | 75.6  | 92.5  | 65.4  | 46.4  | 26.7  | 29.4  | 3.0  | 52.3\nL194: StableLM-3B  | 44.8  | 67.0  | 75.4  | 74.2  | 78.4  | 93.4  | 68.4  | 48.6  | 45.2  | 37.3  | 10.8  | 58.5\nL195: Instella-3B-Stage1  | 53.9  | 73.2  | 78.7  | 74.2  | 77.5  | 94.9  | 71.2  | 51.4  | 54.7  | 34.3  | 10.8  | 61.3\nL196: Instella-3B  | 52.8  | 70.5  | 76.5  | 75.0  | 77.8  | 96.4  | 73.1  | 52.4  | 58.3  | 39.7  | 59.8  | 66.6\nL197: Table 5: Instella 3B base model performance. We report the model performance after stage 1 and stage 2 pretraining. For stage 2, we run the training for three times with different random seeds and merge model weights to obtain the final stage 2 model.\nL198: Models  | ARC-C  | ARC-E  | BoolQ  | HS.  | PiQA  | SciQ  | WG.  | OBQA  | MMLU  | BBH  | GSM8K  | Avg.\nL199: Stage1  | 53.9  | 73.2  | 78.7  | 74.2  | 77.5  | 94.9  | 71.2  | 51.4  | 54.7  | 34.3  | 10.8  | 61.3\nL200: Stage2-seed1  | 51.2  | 68.8  | 76.2  | 73.8  | 77.3  | 96.6  | 72.1  | 52.0  | 57.7  | 38.5  | 56.1  | 65.5\nL201: Stage2-seed2  | 50.8  | 68.4  | 77.8  | 74.3  | 77.2  | 96.6  | 71.8  | 51.4  | 58.2  | 38.5  | 58.8  | 65.8\nL202: Stage2-seed3  | 49.8  | 68.8  | 73.5  | 75.6  | 77.2  | 96.7  | 72.8  | 52.0  | 58.0  | 38.6  | 58.3  | 65.6\nL203: Stage2  | 52.8  | 70.5  | 76.5  | 75.0  | 77.8  | 96.4  | 73.1  | 52.4  | 58.3  | 39.7  | 59.8  | 66.6\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing Instella-Long: A Fully Open Language Model with Long-Context Capability — ROCm Blogs (https://rocm.blogs.amd.com/artificial-intelligence/instella-long-context/README.html)\nciteturn2453view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2451view1\",\"lineno\":43}); Total lines: 235\nL28:   * Supported by the AMD ROCm software stack, Instella-Long employs efficient training techniques such as Sequence Parallelism, FlashAttention-2 cite25†[4] , Torch Compile, and FSDP to distribute model training over 8 MI300 nodes each with 8 GPUs.\nL29: ## Instella-Longcite26†# L30: \nL31: cite16†Instella-Long†huggingface.co is based on the cite27†Instella model released in March. Specifically, Instella-Long is continually trained from cite17†Instella-3B-Instruct†huggingface.co and follows the same model architecture. The training of Instella-Long comprises three stages: 1. Continued Pre-Training, 2. Supervised Finetuning (SFT), 3. Direct Preference Optimization (DPO).\nL32: ## Continued Pre-Trainingcite28†# L33: \nL34: Training: We employ a two-phase pre-training starting from Instella-3B-Instruct (4K context length).\nL35: \nL36: Phase 1: We extend the context length from 4,096 to 65,536 tokens and train the model using 20B tokens. We follow the cite29†RoPE scaling law†www.gradient.ai to increase the base frequency of RoPE cite30†[5] from 10,000 to 514,640.\nL37: Phase 2: As indicated by cite31†Prolong†arxiv.org cite32†[6] , it is beneficial to train the model with the data whose context length is longer than the target context length. In this phase, we train the model on 20B tokens with a maximum context length of 262,144 - 2× the target context length of 128K. Following the RoPE scaling law, we increase the RoPE base frequency to 3,691,950.\nL38: Data: Our continued pre-training data originates from the data mix created by cite31†Prolong†arxiv.org . We use the text data curated by Prolong and tokenize the data with our tokenizer. In each phase of the continued pre-training, we train on a mix of long and short context data. Specific details are outlined as follows:\nL39: Table 1: Continued pre-training data.\nL40: Training Phase  | 64K Long Data  | 256K Long Data  | Short Data\nL41: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL42: Phase 1  | Code repos (30%),\nL43: Books (30%),\nL44: Textbooks (3%)  | -  | FineWeb-Edu (10%),\nL45: FineWeb (10%),\nL46: StackExchange (4%),\nL47: Wikipedia (5%),\nL48: ArXiv (3%),\nL49: OpenWebMath (5%)\nL50: Phase 2  | Code repos (10%),\nL51: Books (15%)  | Code repos (20%),\nL52: Books (15%),\nL53: Textbooks (2%)  | FineWeb-Edu (10%),\nL54: FineWeb (10%),\nL55: StackExchange (4%),\nL56: Wikipedia (5%),\nL57: ArXiv (4%),\nL58: OpenWebMath (5%)\nL59: ## Supervised Finetuning (SFT)cite33†# L60: \nL61: Training: After continued training on the long-context pre-training data, we perform supervised finetuning on long-context instruction data. We train the model using a 1B-token mixture of short- and long-context instruction data.\nL62: Data: Similar to the continued pre-training stage, we train the model on a mixture of short- and long-context instruction data with a ratio of 4 to 6. For short-context instruction data, we use cite34†Ultrachat 200K†huggingface.co cite35†[7] , cite36†OpenMathinstruct-2†huggingface.co cite37†[8] , cite38†Tülu-3 Instruction Following†huggingface.co cite39†[9] , and cite40†MMLU auxiliary train set†huggingface.co cite41†[10] .\nL63: For long-context instruction data, we construct a synthetic long-context instruction dataset due to the lack of long-context SFT data. Specifically, we make use of the long documents from Books, which is part of our continued pre-training data corpus. We select documents with a minimum length of 8K tokens and truncate those exceeding 128K tokens to a maximum length of 128K.\nL64: Then, we use cite42†Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct-1M†huggingface.co as a teacher model to synthetically generate question-answer pairs for the documents. To speed up this process, we randomly choose a subpart of the document for the QA generation instead of using the whole document. The length of the subpart is randomly set to be between 2K and 8K tokens. We use NLTK sentence tokenizer to divide documents into sentences to make sure that the selected subpart has complete sentences.\nL65: The generated question and answer are appended to the end of the long document, serving as a complete single-round instruction-following data sample. In addition, we also generate long-context instruction data using short documents, in order to increase the dataset diversity with more data sources. We use arXiv from our continued pre-training corpus and the DCLM subset from cite43†Dolmino-Mix-1124†huggingface.co cite44†[11] .\nL66: We first generate QA for each short document following the same pipeline aforementioned. Then, we iteratively concatenate different short documents until it reache 128K tokens. The concatenated document can exceed 128K as we do not truncate the last document. Lastly, we randomly choose one QA corresponding to one of the short documents and append it to the end of the concatenated document. The final data mixture for the SFT stage is shown as follows:\nL67: Table 2: Supervised finetuning data.\nL68: Short Data  | Long Data\nL69: --- | ---\nL70: Ultrachat 200K (25%),\nL71: OpenMathinstruct-2, (10%)\nL72: MMLU auxiliary train set (3%),\nL73: Tülu-3 Instruction Following (2%)  | Books (44%),\nL74: DCLM (10%),\nL75: ArXiv (6%)\nL76: ## Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)cite45†# L77: Training: At the last training stage, we perform human preference alignment training using Direct Preference Optimization cite46†[12] . We employ the same DPO training as Instella-3B-Instruct using the same data. Unlike previous training stages, in the DPO stage, we train on short data only whose maximum context length is 2K. Consistent with the findings of other open-weights models, we observe that performing DPO on short data alone continues to improve the model performance on long-context tasks.\nL78: Data: We use the cite47†OLMo-2-1124-7B-Preference-Mix†huggingface.co cite44†[11] dataset as our DPO data which contains 0.76B tokens.\nL79: ## Sequence Parallelismcite48†# L80: \nL81: To enable training with extremely long inputs, we implement sequence parallelism based on Deepspeed Ulysses cite49†[13] . The sequence parallelism distributes the attention heads across GPUs during the attention computation. It is more efficient than Ring-Attention cite50†[14] in GPU communications. We use four GPUs as a sequence parallelism group for the Phase 2 continued pre-training and SFT due to the long inputs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing Instella-Math: Fully Open Language Model with Reasoning Capability — ROCm Blogs (https://rocm.blogs.amd.com/artificial-intelligence/instella-math-language/README.html)\nciteturn2453view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2451view2\",\"lineno\":29}); Total lines: 137\nL6:   * cite0†Supervised Finetuning (SFT) L7:     * cite1†Stage 1: Instruction Tuning with OpenMathInstruct-2 for Mathematical Coverage L8:     * cite2†Stage 2: Deep Reasoning with Long-Context Training on AM-DeepSeek-R1-Distilled L9:   * cite3†Reinforcement Learning (GRPO) L10:     * cite4†Stage 1: GRPO with 8 Rollouts and 8K Output Contexts on Big-Math L11:     * cite5†Stage 2: GRPO with Extended 16 Rollouts and 16K Output Contexts on DeepMath L12:     * cite6†Stage 3: GRPO with Extended 16 Rollouts and 16K Output Contexts on DeepScaleR L13: # Introducing Instella-Math: Fully Open Language Model with Reasoning Capabilitycite7†# L14: \nL15: cite8†Image: Introducing Instella-Math: Fully Open Language Model with Reasoning Capability L16: \nL17: August 09, 2025 by cite9†Xiaodong Yu , cite10†Jiang Liu , cite11†Yusheng Su , cite12†Gowtham Ramesh , cite13†Zicheng Liu , cite14†Prakamya Mishra , cite15†Sudhanshu Ranjan , cite16†Jialian Wu , cite17†Ximeng Sun , cite18†Ze Wang , cite19†Emad Barsoum .\nL18: \nL19: 2 min read. | 475 total words.\nL20: \nL21: cite20†Applications & models L22: \nL23: cite21†AI/ML L24: \nL25: cite22†AI , cite23†Developers L26: AMD is thrilled to introduce cite24†Instella-Math†huggingface.co , a reasoning-focused language model that marks a major milestone for AMD: as far as we know, it’s the first language model trained with long chain-of-thought reinforcement learning entirely on AMD GPUs.\nL27: Starting from cite25†Instella-3B-Instruct†huggingface.co , we extended the model’s capabilities through a multi-stage training pipeline—featuring two stages of supervised fine-tuning and three stages of reinforcement learning using the cite26†VERL framework —executed entirely on AMD Instinct™ MI300X GPUs. This blog offers an inside look at the training process and highlights Instella-Math’s performance on challenging reasoning benchmarks, demonstrating the strength of both the model and the hardware behind it.\nL28: ## Instella-Mathcite27†# L29: Introducing Instella-Math — the first reasoning-centric language model with 3 billion parameters from AMD, fully trained on 32 AMD Instinct MI300X GPUs. cite24†Instella-Math†huggingface.co is a fully open reasoning language model. Its architecture, training code, weights, and datasets are publicly available, allowing anyone to inspect, use, modify, or build upon the model. In addition, its base model, cite25†Instella-3B-Instruct†huggingface.co , is fully open and the reasoning SFT is also fully open.\nL30: Built on the AMD ROCm™ software stack, Instella-Math leverages efficient distributed training techniques, including reinforcement learning across 4 MI300X nodes (8 GPUs each), demonstrating the scalability and performance of AMD hardware for cutting-edge AI workloads. Derived from Instella-3B-Instruct with an identical architecture, Instella-Math is optimized for multi-step logical reasoning, mathematical problem-solving, and chain-of-thought tasks.\nL31: The training pipeline features two stages of supervised fine-tuning followed by three reinforcement learning stages using the GRPO algorithm, as shown in Figure 1.\nL32: cite28†Image: Scaling performance L33: \nL34: Figure 1. Instella-Math Training Stepscite29†# L35: ## Supervised Finetuning (SFT)cite0†# L36: \nL37: We perform a two-stage supervised fine-tuning process to gradually enhance the reasoning capabilities of the Instella-3B-Instruct model. In the first stage we use instruction tuning for mathematical coverage. The second stage enables the model to generate in-depth analyses and structured reasoning steps, which are crucial for tackling complex problems like Olympiad-level math questions.\nL38: ### Stage 1: Instruction Tuning with OpenMathInstruct-2 for Mathematical Coveragecite1†# L39: In the first stage of SFT, we begin with instruction tuning, following instructions or prompts properly, especially in a question-answer or problem-solution format. Using the cite30†OpenMathInstruct-2†huggingface.co dataset, which consists of 14 million problem-solution pairs generated from the GSM8K and MATH training sets, the model is trained to follow mathematical prompts covering a diverse range of topics from arithmetic and algebra to probability and calculus.\nL40: ### Stage 2: Deep Reasoning with Long-Context Training on AM-DeepSeek-R1-Distilledcite2†# L41: In the second SFT stage, we further improve the model’s reasoning capability by training on cite31†AM-DeepSeek-R1-Distilled-1.4M†huggingface.co , which is a large-scale general reasoning dataset containing high-quality and challenging problems. In this stage, we increase the context length of the model from 4K to 32K to allow the model to learn from the long chain-of-thought responses distilled from large reasoning models such as DeepSeek-R1.\nL42: ## Reinforcement Learning (GRPO)cite3†# L43: ### Stage 1: GRPO with 8 Rollouts and 8K Output Contexts on Big-Mathcite4†# L44: Training: In the first stage of reinforcement learning, we apply the Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) algorithm to train the model on cite32†Big-Math-RL-Verified†huggingface.co , a curated set of complex multi-step math problems. We generate 8 rollouts per prompt, each allowing up to 8K output tokens, to explore diverse reasoning trajectories. The model is trained for 1,200 GRPO steps, using rule-based reward signals designed by Prime-RL that favor correctness of solutions in the desired format.\nL45: Training is distributed over 16 MI300X GPUs across 2 nodes, with VERL and VLLM enabling stable and efficient rollout collection, reward evaluation, and policy updates.\nL46: ### Stage 2: GRPO with Extended 16 Rollouts and 16K Output Contexts on DeepMathcite5†# L47: Training: To push the limits of long-form reasoning, we conduct a second GRPO stage on cite33†DeepMath†huggingface.co using 16 rollouts per prompt with up to 16K output tokens. This stage is designed to maximize the model’s capacity for deep mathematical reasoning, enabling it to solve problems that require extended derivations, multiple nested logical steps, or structured proof-like outputs.\nL48: In this stage, training is distributed over 32 MI300X GPUs across 4 nodes, and the model is trained for 600 GRPO steps.\nL49: ### Stage 3: GRPO with Extended 16 Rollouts and 16K Output Contexts on DeepScaleRcite6†# "
